  "notes": "John Russell Pope's West Building (1941), from its published plan and dimensions (about 239 m long): two long windowless wings of pink Tennessee marble either side of a taller central block under the rotunda's low dome and oculus; four skylit garden courts; the galleries' skylight strips down the roofs; the Ionic portico and steps on the Mall (south), the smaller porch on Constitution Avenue (north); end and mid-wing pavilions. Guessed: the exact pavilion widths, the court sizes, the column count on the north porch. No photographs were available in the session (Wikimedia blocked); refine from photographs. Refined 24 Sep 2026 without photographs, from the published descriptions (SAH Archipedia, NGA campus page, Docomomo): the Mall portico has twelve Ionic columns in two rows, the inner ones framing three doors - the inner row of four is added and the portico's back wall is now marble with three dark doorways between them (it was one black band); length 782 ft (238 m) confirms the plan; rose-white Tennessee marble is drawn as marble. The low dome over the rotunda (modelled on the Pantheon) now has two stepped marble rings at its foot. No photographs were available."
